  "worked_solution": "Clearly, $b\\ne 0.$ Substituting $x=\\frac{1}{b}$ into the equation of the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =k{log}_{2}⁡(b\\cdot \\frac{1}{b}) \\\\  & =k{log}_{2}⁡1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the point $(\\frac{1}{b},0)$ lies on the curve. But from the diagram, we know that $(3,0)$ lies on the curve. Hence\n\n$$\n(3,0)=(\\frac{1}{b},0)\\,⇒\\,b=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute coordinates of the point $(6,2)$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{log}_{2}⁡(bx) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}x) & =y \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 6) & =2 \\\\ k{log}_{2}⁡2 & =2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nk\\cdot b=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$",
